Subject: [PATCH] bootm: refuse framebuffer writes in bootm_load_os()
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [local diagnostic]

bootm_load_os() makes two writes into addresses taken from the OS image
metadata and the arm64 Image header without comparing either of them with the
framebuffer reservation recorded by video_reserve():

  1. image_decomp() writes the decompressed OS image straight into the load
     address. The only output bound bootm_load_os() gives the decompressor is
     CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN, so that declared window - not the actual, unknown
     uncompressed size and not any smaller allocation - is what this guard
     checks before the write.
  2. The optional ARM64 relocation memmoves exactly image_size bytes, the
     "effective image size" read from the arm64 Image header by
     booti_setup(), to relocated_addr. That is an exact length, so the exact
     window is checked before the memmove.

Both checks use only gd_video_bottom()/gd_video_top() and wrap-safe
arithmetic, in the same shape as the booti guard in patch 15. A partially set
or inverted recorded video range fails closed, a destination whose end address
would wrap is refused, and no window is checked when nothing is written.

Per compression type, the window checked for write 1 is:

  - IH_COMP_NONE: exactly image_len bytes. image.c copies with memmove_wd()
    only when image_len <= unc_len, and returns -ENOSPC without writing
    otherwise, which is reported as a refusal here.
  - IH_COMP_GZIP: at most unc_len bytes (lib/zlib/inflate.c limits every
    literal and match copy to avail_out, which zunzip() sets from the length
    it is given). lib/gunzip.c takes that length as an int, so a
    CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN above INT_MAX would narrow - or negate - the bound
    before zlib sees it; such a value is refused instead of being checked with
    a window narrower than the one that would be used.
  - IH_COMP_BZIP2: at most unc_len bytes. lib/bzip2/bzlib.c passes *destLen
    to BZ2_bzDecompress() as avail_out and reports BZ_OUTBUFF_FULL instead of
    running past it.
  - IH_COMP_LZO: at most unc_len bytes. lib/lzo/lzo1x_decompress.c tracks the
    bytes left in the destination and returns LZO_E_OUTPUT_OVERRUN before
    writing a block that does not fit; lzo1x_decompress_safe() bounds each
    copy with HAVE_OP().
  - IH_COMP_LZMA: at most unc_len bytes. lib/lzma/LzmaTools.c returns
    SZ_ERROR_OUTPUT_EOF before decompressing when the size declared in the
    LZMA header exceeds the buffer, and never processes more than the buffer
    holds.
  - IH_COMP_LZ4: at most unc_len bytes. lib/lz4_wrapper.c bounds every block
    with the remaining destination capacity and stops with -ENOBUFS.
  - IH_COMP_ZSTD: at most unc_len bytes. lib/zstd/zstd.c hands the buffer as
    the frame destination capacity, which is an error, not a longer write,
    when it is too small.

Any other compression type has no in-tree implementation this guard can bound,
so it is refused before image_decomp() runs.

An uncompressed image that is already at its load address is not copied at
all - image_decomp() returns early - so no window is checked for it and a
malformed video range does not block that boot. For a KERNEL_NOLOAD image the
decompression window deliberately starts at the freshly allocated buffer and
is unc_len long: image_decomp() is told CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN bytes are
available even when ALIGN(image_len * 4, SZ_1M) allocated less, so the check
must not be narrower than what the decompressor was told.

The check is against the widest window the decompressor may write, so a
refusal can be conservative: an image that would have decompressed into a
smaller window is refused just the same.

Scope of this diagnostic:

- Only the two writes above are checked, and only against the recorded video
  reservation. No other reserved memory, LMB region, load address, source
  address or image-header field is revalidated, and nothing is reserved,
  relocated or decompressed differently.
- The existing legacy/new-format overwrite check on the OS blob and the LMB
  allocation that follows decompression are unchanged and keep their place in
  the sequence: the guard is added immediately before each write.
- Not covered: the pre-loading of FIT/bootm payloads (bootm_find_images and
  the FIT loader write the kernel, initrd and FDT before bootm_load_os() is
  reached), raw load/acp commands, initrd and FDT placement, EFI loaders, the
  other bootmeths, extlinux reads (patch 14 covers only that reader) and an
  active IOMMU during handoff. Nothing here is evidence that handoff is safe.
- The cache maintenance that follows the decompression (flush_cache over the
  decompressed range) and the LMB bookkeeping are not payload writes and are
  not checked; in the no-copy case that flush still spans the source image
  without any window check.
- A refusal prints a guard message and returns BOOTM_ERR_RESET, the same
  "reset to recover" outcome as a failed decompression.

Compiled only with the opt-in WIP configuration; not release code.

---
--- a/boot/bootm.c
+++ b/boot/bootm.c
@@ -15,5 +15,6 @@
 #include <irq_func.h>
 #include <lmb.h>
+#include <limits.h>
 #include <log.h>
 #include <malloc.h>
 #include <mapmem.h>
@@ -44,6 +45,118 @@
 
 DECLARE_GLOBAL_DATA_PTR;
 
+/*
+ * Arcadia local diagnostic: would a write cover part of the framebuffer
+ * reservation recorded by video_reserve()?
+ *
+ * The decision uses only gd_video_bottom()/gd_video_top(), so it needs no
+ * driver or devicetree access and is a no-op when video reserved nothing.
+ * It protects that single range only; other reserved memory, the LMB map and
+ * the boot payload itself are unchanged.
+ *
+ * @addr:	address the write starts at
+ * @size:	number of bytes the write covers
+ * Return:	0 to allow the write, -ERANGE for an unusable video range or a
+ *		wrapping destination, -EPERM if the destination overlaps video
+ */
+static int arcadia_bootm_video_write_check(ulong addr, ulong size)
+{
+	ulong bottom = gd_video_bottom();
+	ulong top = gd_video_top();
+
+	/* No video reservation: nothing to protect, so leave the write alone. */
+	if (!bottom && !top)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* A partial or inverted range cannot be checked, so fail closed. */
+	if (!bottom || top <= bottom)
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	/* A zero-length write covers no bytes. */
+	if (!size)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* Reject a destination end address that would wrap. */
+	if (addr + size < addr)
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	/* Writes wholly below or wholly above the reservation are unaffected. */
+	if (addr >= top || addr + size <= bottom)
+		return 0;
+
+	return -EPERM;
+}
+
+/**
+ * arcadia_bootm_decomp_guard() - bound the bootm decompression write window
+ * @comp:	compression type recorded for the OS image
+ * @load:	address image_decomp() decompresses into
+ * @image_start: address the image is read from
+ * @image_len:	image length recorded by the image parser
+ * @unc_len:	CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN, the output size handed to image_decomp()
+ *
+ * bootm_load_os() decompresses straight into the load address, and it tells
+ * image_decomp() that @unc_len bytes are available. That declared size, not
+ * any smaller allocation, is the only bound this guard can assert without
+ * knowing the actual uncompressed length in advance. Per compression type:
+ *
+ * - IH_COMP_NONE copies exactly @image_len bytes, or nothing at all when
+ *   @image_len exceeds @unc_len (image.c then returns -ENOSPC unwritten).
+ * - IH_COMP_GZIP writes at most @unc_len bytes, but lib/gunzip.c passes the
+ *   length to zlib as an int. A value that does not land in the positive
+ *   range is refused rather than checked with the wrong bound.
+ * - IH_COMP_BZIP2, IH_COMP_LZO, IH_COMP_LZMA, IH_COMP_LZ4 and IH_COMP_ZSTD
+ *   write at most @unc_len bytes: each in-tree implementation receives that
+ *   size as its output capacity and reports an error instead of running
+ *   past it.
+ * - Any other type cannot be bounded here, so it is refused.
+ *
+ * The check is against the widest window the decompressor may write, so a
+ * refusal can be conservative: an image that would have decompressed into a
+ * smaller window is refused just the same.
+ *
+ * Return:	0 if the write (if any) misses the recorded video reservation,
+ *		-EPERM if it would cover part of it, -ERANGE for a window that
+ *		cannot be expressed, -ENOSPC for an uncompressed image larger
+ *		than the buffer and -ENOTSUPP for an unbounded compression type
+ */
+static int arcadia_bootm_decomp_guard(int comp, ulong load, ulong image_start,
+				      ulong image_len, ulong unc_len)
+{
+	ulong size;
+
+	/*
+	 * image_decomp() writes nothing when an uncompressed image is already
+	 * at its load address, so there is no write window to check here.
+	 */
+	if (comp == IH_COMP_NONE && load == image_start)
+		return 0;
+
+	switch (comp) {
+	case IH_COMP_NONE:
+		if (image_len > unc_len)
+			return -ENOSPC;
+		size = image_len;
+		break;
+	case IH_COMP_GZIP:
+		if (unc_len > INT_MAX)
+			return -ERANGE;
+		size = unc_len;
+		break;
+	case IH_COMP_BZIP2:
+	case IH_COMP_LZO:
+	case IH_COMP_LZMA:
+	case IH_COMP_LZ4:
+	case IH_COMP_ZSTD:
+		size = unc_len;
+		break;
+	default:
+		return -ENOTSUPP;
+	}
+
+	return arcadia_bootm_video_write_check(load, size);
+}
+
 struct bootm_headers images;		/* pointers to os/initrd/fdt images */
 
 __weak void board_quiesce_devices(void)
@@ -649,6 +762,20 @@
 
 	load_buf = map_sysmem(load, 0);
 	image_buf = map_sysmem(os.image_start, image_len);
+	/*
+	 * Arcadia: bound the decompression write before image_decomp() runs.
+	 * Refusing here leaves the board needing a reset, exactly like a
+	 * failed decompression.
+	 */
+	err = arcadia_bootm_decomp_guard(os.comp, load, image_start, image_len,
+					 CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN);
+	if (err) {
+		printf("Arcadia bootm guard: decompress %lx bound %lx, video %lx-%lx err %d\n",
+		       load, (ulong)CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN, gd_video_bottom(),
+		       gd_video_top(), err);
+		return BOOTM_ERR_RESET;
+	}
+
 	err = image_decomp(os.comp, load, os.image_start, os.type,
 			   load_buf, image_buf, image_len,
 			   CONFIG_SYS_BOOTM_LEN, &load_end);
@@ -701,6 +828,19 @@
 
 		/* Handle BOOTM_STATE_LOADOS */
 		if (relocated_addr != load) {
+			/*
+			 * Arcadia: the relocation copies exactly image_size
+			 * bytes from the Image header, so check that window
+			 * before the memmove writes it.
+			 */
+			ret = arcadia_bootm_video_write_check(relocated_addr,
+							     image_size);
+			if (ret) {
+				printf("Arcadia bootm guard: relocate %lx size %lx, video %lx-%lx err %d\n",
+				       relocated_addr, image_size,
+				       gd_video_bottom(), gd_video_top(), ret);
+				return BOOTM_ERR_RESET;
+			}
 			printf("Moving Image from 0x%lx to 0x%lx, end=0x%lx\n",
 			       load, relocated_addr,
 			       relocated_addr + image_size);
